description:
## useEffect & Lifecycle

`useEffect` lets you run **side effects** — things that happen outside of rendering, like updating the document title, setting up timers, or fetching data.

### How It Works
```jsx
// Runs after every render
useEffect(() => { ... });

// Runs only on mount (empty deps)
useEffect(() => { ... }, []);

// Runs when "count" changes
useEffect(() => { ... }, [count]);

// Cleanup function (runs before next effect or unmount)
useEffect(() => {
  const timer = setInterval(...);
  return () => clearInterval(timer); // cleanup
}, []);
```

### Your Task
1. Create a counter with `useState`, starting at **0**
2. Display it in `<h2 id="count">`
3. Use `useEffect` to update `document.title` to `"Count: X"` whenever count changes
4. Add an **Increment** button with id `"increment"`
5. Display a message `<p id="mounted">` that says **"Component is mounted!"** — set this text using `useEffect` with an empty dependency array and a state variable

> **Note:** The document title won't be visible in the preview, but the tests will check it!

src/App.jsx.contents:
import React, { useState, useEffect, useRef, useMemo, useCallback, useReducer, createContext, useContext, Component } from 'react';

function App() {
  const [count, setCount] = useState(0);
  const [mountMsg, setMountMsg] = useState('');

  // Add your useEffect hooks here

  return (
    <div>
      <h2 id="count">Count: {count}</h2>
      <button id="increment" onClick={() => setCount(count + 1)}>
        Increment
      </button>
      <p id="mounted">{mountMsg}</p>
    </div>
  );
}

export default App;

